# Nous base.manifest — nous's module declaration for the weave layer compiler.
#
# nous is BOTH a consumer and a base: it consumes ariadne as its substrate
# (construct/deps `substrate ../ariadne`) and inherits ariadne's EXPORTED base
# layer through that edge, AND it exports its OWN artifacts (the nous-* skills,
# Makefile.nous, directory conventions) down to its consumers (brain). This file
# declares only nous's own contributions; ariadne's layer is walked first as a
# transitive ancestor.
#
# Format and verbs are documented in ariadne's construct/base.manifest. The
# OPTIONAL leading visibility token (ariadne#99 composition algebra) tags a row
# `export` (flows down to consumers — the default when omitted) or `internal`
# (stays with nous: selected only on nous's own self-walk, never leaked into a
# consumer's composition).

# ── Constitution ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# nous's own local extensions. weave concatenates this fragment into nous's
# composed AGENTS.md AFTER every ancestor's exported prose (ariadne's exported
# AGENTS.base.md / Constitution). Declared `internal` so it composes ONLY on
# nous's own self-walk and never leaks DOWN into a consumer's AGENTS.md — and,
# conversely, ariadne's own internal AGENTS.local.md never leaks into nous's.
# This is the structural fix for the #95 bug (the old `symlink AGENTS.md` whose
# @-import silently resolved to ariadne's local file).
internal  prose AGENTS.local.md

# ── Nous skills ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# nous-tools is the consolidated skill: covers both tool invocation (Audience
# A — invoke cmd/<name>) and tool authoring (Audience B — write code that
# uses the proxy via `nous instructions`). The standalone charon skill was
# retired after charon merged into the nous binary; its protocol-guide
# content lives inside nous itself, accessed via `nous instructions`.
symlink   construct/skills/nous-tools    .claude/skills/nous-tools
symlink   construct/skills/nous-resolve  .claude/skills/nous-resolve

# ── Makefile ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
symlink   Makefile.nous

# ── Documentation ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
symlink   README.md           README-NOUS.md

# ── Directory conventions ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
scaffold  life
scaffold  memory/ideas/pensive
scaffold  memory/life/travel
scaffold  memory/life/references
scaffold  memory/work/meeting-notes

# ── Plugins (merged from the former nous/plugins/*.manifest pattern) ─────────
# Plugin manifests were a pre-2026-05-19 modular packaging mechanism. With
# selective-plugin support gone (every plugin always applied), the
# modularity adds no operator-visible value, so we inline the contents
# here. Adding new "plugin-shaped" contributions: just append symlink
# entries.

# gmail / oneshot (and lib/gmail) are nous-owned Go binaries. Per the
# ownership = location convention (ariadne#57) they are NOT source-distributed
# to derivatives — derivatives obtain them as built binaries (the dev-alias
# build-in-owner mechanism, or a built bin/), never symlinked source. The
# former `symlink lib/gmail` / `symlink cmd/gmail` / `symlink cmd/oneshot`
# directives are retired (code flows through Go modules, not the file-symlink
# substrate channel, which stays for docs/config only).
#   gmail:   search/read Gmail threads via the nous proxy (`nous auth google <email>`).
#   oneshot: single system+user prompt against a local Ollama model (ollama serve).
